Abstract

We present a consolidated formal research architecture (v2.0) for the sequential deployment of payloads from reusable launch vehicles (RLVs), instantiated as a case study on the SpaceX Starship "Pez Dispenser" system. The v2.0 revision incorporates the findings of a deep internet search adversarial review that identified structural defects in the v1.0 evidence-theoretic layer, the TLA⁺ specification, and the physical-model uncertainty propagation. We reformulate the copula-based dependence combination as a cumulative-mass construction following the random-set approach to belief-function joining, replace the β-factor heuristic with an explicit correlation-adjusted conservative fusion rule, and enlarge the frame of discernment to render the DSmT/PCR apparatus non-trivial. We introduce an adversarial Environment action in the TLA⁺ module, instantiate the evidential constants as state functions, resolve the terminal-payload deadlock, and repair the t_hold invariant scoping. We provide a corrected treatment of the collision-probability threshold as a screening criterion rather than an authorization criterion, and scope the FCC 5-year rule to satellites launched on or after 29 September 2024. We conclude with a claim ledger, an open-problem register, and an explicit falsification protocol.

Keywords: payload deployment, fault tolerance, evidence theory, DSmT, formal verification, TLA⁺, random sets, copulas, imprecise probabilities, safety kernel, correlated observability, reusable launch vehicles

