2. Related Work

2.1 Historical Separation Failures

Guernsey et al. (AAS 03-071) report that from 1983 to 2005, spacecraft and fairing separation systems accounted for approximately 10% of all launch failures. This establishes separation as a non-negligible hazard class.

2.2 Fault-Tolerant Separation Systems

Traditional separation systems employ pyrotechnic redundancy with Single Failure Tolerant (SFT) philosophy. NASA HR-31 states that "the space system shall provide at least single failure tolerance to catastrophic events, with specific levels of failure tolerance and implementation (similar or dissimilar redundancy) derived via an integration of the design and safety analysis." Critically, NASA guidance adds that "redundancy alone does not meet the intent of this requirement. When a critical system fails because of improper or unexpected performance due to unanticipated conditions, similar redundancy can be ineffective at preventing the complete loss of the system." Dissimilar redundancy "can be very effective provided there is sufficient separation among the redundant legs."

2.3 Formal Methods in Space Systems

NASA's Gateway Vehicle System Manager (VSM) employs assume-guarantee contracts implemented using the PlusCal/TLA⁺ environment for developmental verification, with functionality represented as a state machine and contracts implemented as temporal formulas evaluated using model checkers such as TLC. Runtime assume-guarantee contracts are implemented in R2U2 via a runtime monitor. Assume-guarantee contracts define expected behavior of systems, subsystems, and components, with assumptions or pre-conditions on the subsystem's inputs bounding the conditions in which the contract applies. The DLR has demonstrated symbolic model checking scalability to 250 state machines.

2.4 Evidence Theory, Correlation, and Conflict

Dempster–Shafer theory (DST) has been applied to preliminary mission design under uncertainty and conjunction analysis with epistemic uncertainty. However, DST requires independence: classical evidence theory assumes that bodies of evidence are independent. Dempster's rule can only be applied to independent bodies of evidence.

The literature distinguishes two orthogonal pathologies in evidence fusion:

· Conflict: mutually inconsistent evidence, addressed by PCR5/PCR6 and related redistribution rules.
· Correlation: statistically dependent sources, addressed by random-set theory, copulas, or β-factor decomposition.

The PCR5 (resp. PCR6) rule of combination is not associative, which means that the fusion of the BBAs must be done using the general formula if one has more than two BBAs to combine. The sequential PCR5 (resp. PCR6) combination of $S > 2$ BBAs are not in general equal to the global PCR5 (resp. PCR6) fusion of the $S$ BBAs altogether because the order of the combination of the sources does matter. PCR6 is simpler to implement than PCR5 and is better than PCR5 for decision-making when $S > 2$. The PCR6 rule is quasi-associative and it offers a more refined conflict redistribution than the DS rule.

2.5 Random Sets, Copulas, and Belief Functions

Random sets are random variables whose values are sets; they induce a set of probability measures, and the resulting lower and upper probabilities correspond to belief and plausibility functions in Dempster–Shafer theory. Copulas can be used to describe the dependence between random sets. It has been proven that a joint belief function is related to its marginal belief functions by a family of copulas; in general, a single copula is not sufficient. Joining belief functions can be done by replacing the CDFs with cumulated masses: the joint mass is computed using the $C$-volume of the copula on the partition of the unit hypercube generated by the marginal focal elements. Applying the copula directly to the lower probabilities does not produce a coherent lower probability inducing a non-empty credal set.

2.6 Gap

Within the literature reviewed for this preliminary study, we did not identify a single framework that jointly addresses: (a) sequential constellation deployment, (b) evidence-theoretic authorization under correlated observability with explicit conflict–correlation separation, (c) a formally verified safety kernel with authorization reset between payloads, and (d) constellation-aware state mutation with uncertainty propagation. This paper addresses that gap.

