3.4 Mass and Inertia Mutation with Uncertainty Propagation

Between deployments $i$ and $i+1$, the correct mutation accounts for the removal of a finite mass at a specific location. Let the removed satellite have mass $m_s$, position $r_s$ in the body frame, and inertia $I_s^{\text{cm}}$ about its own center of mass.

Step 1 — Translate removed-satellite inertia to old CG.

\mathbf{I}_s^{\text{old}} = \mathbf{I}_s^{\text{cm}} + m_s \left[ (\mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}}^\top \mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}}) \mathbf{1} - \mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}} \mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}}^\top \right], \qquad \mathbf{r}_{s/\text{old}} = \mathbf{r}_s - \mathbf{r}_{\text{CG}}.

Step 2 — Subtract from total inertia about old CG.

\mathbf{I}_{\text{remaining}}^{\text{old}} = \mathbf{I}_{\text{CG}} - \mathbf{I}_s^{\text{old}}.

Step 3 — Translate remaining body to new CG.

New center of mass:

\mathbf{r}'_{\text{CG}} = \frac{m \mathbf{r}_{\text{CG}} - m_s \mathbf{r}_s}{m'}, \qquad m' = m - m_s.

Parallel-axis correction:

\mathbf{I}'_{\text{CG}} = \mathbf{I}_{\text{remaining}}^{\text{old}} + m' \left[ (\mathbf{d}^\top \mathbf{d}) \mathbf{1} - \mathbf{d} \mathbf{d}^\top \right], \qquad \mathbf{d} = \mathbf{r}_{\text{CG}} - \mathbf{r}'_{\text{CG}}.

Uncertainty propagation. Let $\theta = (m_s, r_s, \text{vec } I_s^{\text{cm}})$ be the satellite parameter vector with covariance $\Sigma_\theta$. To first order:

\mathbf{\Sigma}_{I'} \approx \mathbf{J}_{I'} \, \mathbf{\Sigma}_\theta \, \mathbf{J}_{I'}^\top, \qquad \mathbf{J}_{I'} = \frac{\partial \, \text{vec} \, \mathbf{I}'_{\text{CG}}}{\partial \, \mathbf{\theta}}.

Caveat. No public probabilistic model of $(m_s, r_s, I_s^{\text{cm}})$ exists for a deployed flatellite. This propagation is formal, not operational, and is logged as Open Problem OP8.

