4. Epistemic Framework (Revised)

4.1 Enlarged Frame of Discernment

The v1.0 frame $\Theta = \{S, H\}$ collapsed the evidence-theoretic apparatus: DSmT with the free DSm model was identical to DST, PCR5 reduced to a two-term redistribution, and Bel/Pl reduced to $m(S)$ and $1 - m(S)$. We therefore adopt:

\Theta = \{\text{SAFE}, \text{MECH}, \text{THERM}, \text{DEB}, \text{IGN}\},

where SAFE denotes safe deployment, MECH denotes mechanical hazard (separation mechanism failure, partial separation), THERM denotes thermal hazard (bay over-temperature, hold thermal runaway), DEB denotes debris hazard (post-separation collision, bay exposure), and IGN denotes ignorance (the frame itself). This frame is non-exclusive in the DSmT free model, permitting paradoxical mass assignments that DST would forbid.

4.2 Observation-to-Mass Mapping

Each source $i$ observes a random vector $O_i \in \mathcal{O}_i$. The mass function is the image of an observation-to-mass map

g_i : \mathcal{O}_i \rightarrow \Delta(2^\Theta), \qquad m_i = g_i(O_i).

Thus $m_i$ is a random variable on the observation space. Correlation between sources is a property of the joint distribution of $(O_i, O_j)$, not of the mass functions directly.

4.3 Correlation Model on Observation Residuals

Definition 4.1 (Observation Residual). Let $\bar{O}_i(t)$ be the predicted observation from a nominal model. Define

r_i(t) = O_i(t) - \bar{O}_i(t).

Definition 4.2 (Residual Correlation).

\rho_{ij} = \frac{\text{Cov}(r_i, r_j)}{\sigma_{r_i} \, \sigma_{r_j}}.

Definition 4.3 (Propagation to Mass Functions). If the maps $g_i$ are differentiable, then to first order:

\text{Cov}\big(m_i(A), m_j(A)\big) \approx \frac{\partial g_i}{\partial O_i} \, \Sigma_{O_i, O_j} \, \frac{\partial g_j}{\partial O_j}^{\top}.

4.4 Correlation-Adjusted Conservative Fusion

The v1.0 β-factor model used $m_{\text{common}}(A) = \min\{m_i(A), m_j(A)\}$, which is a sub-probability, not a mass function, and which under-represents HAZARD as well as SAFE. We replace it with the following.

Definition 4.4 (Conservative Correlation Adjustment). When $|\rho_{ij}| > \rho_{\text{threshold}}$, define

m_{ij}^{\text{cons}}(A) = (1 - \beta_{ij}) \, m_{\text{indep}}(A) + \beta_{ij} \, m_{\text{ign}}(A),

where $m_{\text{ign}}$ is the vacuous mass function $m_{\text{ign}}(\Theta) = 1$, and $\beta_{ij} = |\rho_{ij}|$. This is a mass function for all $\beta_{ij} \in [0,1]$, and it monotonically shifts mass toward ignorance as correlation increases. The resulting belief and plausibility functions are well-defined:

\text{Bel}(A) = (1 - \beta_{ij}) \, \text{Bel}_{\text{indep}}(A), \qquad \text{Pl}(A) = (1 - \beta_{ij}) \, \text{Pl}_{\text{indep}}(A) + \beta_{ij}.

The conservative bias is explicit: as $\beta_{ij} \to 1$, $\text{Bel}(S) \to 0$ and $\text{Pl}(H) \to 1$.

4.5 Dependence-Aware Combination via Cumulative Masses

The v1.0 copula combination applied the copula pointwise to the mass functions, which is a category error: a copula is a joint CDF, not a mass. The correct construction, following the random-set approach, replaces the CDFs by cumulated masses. Given marginal belief functions $m_X, m_Y$ with focal sets $\{A_1, \ldots, A_n\}$ and $\{B_1, \ldots, B_m\}$ ordered arbitrarily, define the cumulative mass:

F_X(A_k) = \sum_{j=1}^{k} m_X(A_j), \qquad F_Y(B_\ell) = \sum_{j=1}^{\ell} m_Y(B_j).

For a copula $C_\theta$ on $[0,1]^2$, the joint mass on $A_k \times B_\ell$ is computed as the $C$-volume:

m_{XY}(A_k, B_\ell) = \Delta_{F_X(A_{k-1})}^{F_X(A_k)} \Delta_{F_Y(B_{\ell-1})}^{F_Y(B_\ell)} C_\theta(u, v),

where $\Delta$ denotes the second-order finite difference. The resulting $m_{XY}$ is a valid joint mass function on $2^\Theta \times 2^\Theta$. The marginalization to a single frame is performed by $m_{X \oplus Y}(Z) = \sum_{A \cap B = Z} m_{XY}(A, B)$. This construction produces a belief function, and the resulting lower and upper probabilities are belief and plausibility functions.

Caveat. In general, a single copula is not sufficient to characterize a joint belief function; the result is a family of belief functions parameterized by the copula. This is a feature, not a bug: it is the honest representation of what correlated evidence licenses. The credal set is the family $\{ \text{Bel}_{C_\theta} : \theta \in \Theta_{\text{copula}} \}$.

4.6 Dempster Combination (Independence Regime)

When independence holds:

(m_i \oplus m_j)(A) = \frac{1}{1-K} \sum_{B \cap C = A} m_i(B) m_j(C), \qquad K = \sum_{B \cap C = \emptyset} m_i(B) m_j(C).

4.7 DSmT with PCR6 (High-Conflict, Multi-Source Regime)

For high-conflict scenarios with $S > 2$ sources, PCR6 is preferred over PCR5: PCR5 and PCR6 coincide for two sources, but PCR6 is more stable and simpler to implement for $S > 2$. The PCR6 rule is quasi-associative, and the general formula must be used when fusing more than two BBAs, because the sequential combination is order-dependent. The general PCR6 formula for $S$ sources is:

m_{\text{PCR6}}(X) = m_{1,2,\ldots,S}(X) + \sum_{\substack{i \in \{1,\ldots,S\} \\ X_{j_i} = A}} \sum_{\substack{(X_1,\ldots,X_S) \in (2^\Theta)^S \\ \bigcap_{k=1}^S X_k = \emptyset}} \prod_{k=1}^S m_k(X_k) \cdot \frac{m_i(X_i)^2}{\sum_{k=1}^S m_k(X_k)}.

Drawback. PCR6 is not associative and can become computationally intractable for large $S$ and large frames. The vacuous BBA is not a neutral element for $S > 2$. These are acknowledged limitations.

4.8 Continuous Conflict-and-Correlation Measure

\Gamma(O) = w_K \, \frac{K}{K_{\text{max}}} + w_\rho \, \max_{i<j} \frac{|\rho_{ij}|}{\rho_{\text{max}}}, \qquad w_K + w_\rho = 1.

ABORT is triggered when $\Gamma(O) \geq \Gamma_{\text{max}} \in (0,1]$.

4.9 Decision Rule

The Deployment Safety Kernel produces:

A = \begin{cases} \text{AUTHORIZE} & \text{Bel}(\text{SAFE}) \geq \theta_N \;\land\; \text{Pl}(\text{HAZ}) \leq \theta_H \;\land\; m(\Theta) \leq \theta_U \\ \text{INHIBIT} & \text{Bel}(\text{HAZ}) \geq \theta_H \\ \text{HOLD} & \neg \text{AUTHORIZE} \;\land\; \neg \text{INHIBIT} \;\land\; t_{\text{hold}} < t_{\text{hold}}^{\text{max}} \\ \text{ABORT} & t_{\text{hold}} \geq t_{\text{hold}}^{\text{max}} \;\lor\; \Gamma(O) \geq \Gamma_{\text{max}} \end{cases}

where $\text{HAZ} = \Theta \setminus \{\text{SAFE}\}$.

4.10 Theorem 4.1 (Corrected)

Theorem. If $m(\Theta) > 0$ and $\theta_N + \theta_H < 1$, then $\text{Bel}(S) \geq \theta_N$ does not imply $\text{Pl}(H) \leq \theta_H$.

Proof. $\text{Bel}(S) = m(S) \leq 1 - m(\Theta) < 1$. $\text{Pl}(H) = m(H) + m(\Theta) = 1 - m(S)$. If $\text{Bel}(S) \geq \theta_N$, then $\text{Pl}(H) \leq 1 - \theta_N$. The implication $\text{Pl}(H) \leq \theta_H$ holds if and only if $1 - \theta_N \leq \theta_H$, i.e., $\theta_N + \theta_H \geq 1$. Therefore, when $\theta_N + \theta_H < 1$, the implication fails. $\blacksquare$

Corollary. The threshold condition $\theta_N + \theta_H < 1$ is necessary for the decision rule to be non-vacuous.

4.11 HOLD Bound

t_{\text{hold}}^{\text{max}} = \min\left( \frac{E_{\text{avail}} - E_{\text{reserve}}}{P_{\text{hold}}}, \;\; \frac{T_{\text{max}} - T_{\text{current}}}{\dot{T}_{\text{net}}}, \;\; T_{\text{window}}(t) \right) - \epsilon,

with guards for $P_{\text{hold}} = 0$ and $\dot{T}_{\text{net}} \leq 0$ as in v1.0. The bound is recomputed continuously and clamped to $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$.

