5.2 Collision Assessment (Corrected)

Before deployment $i+1$, for all $j \in \{1, \ldots, k\}$:

P_c(x_{i+1}, x_j) \leq P_{c,\text{threshold}}.

The v1.0 document treated $P_{c,\text{threshold}} = 10^{-4}$ as a "widely accepted" deployment criterion. This is a mis-scoping. At NASA CARA, $P_c$ values greater than $10^{-7}$ merit operational attention, and values greater than $10^{-4}$ require mitigation actions (maneuver decisions) per NPR 8079.1. The $10^{-4}$ figure is a screening and maneuver-decision threshold, not a deployment authorization criterion. For deployment authorization, the relevant criterion is the aggregate collision risk over the constellation lifetime, which is a different quantity.

Reformulated criterion. Deployment of payload $i+1$ is authorized only if:

\max_{j} P_c(x_{i+1}, x_j) < P_{c,\text{screen}} \quad \land \quad \sum_{j} P_c(x_{i+1}, x_j) < P_{c,\text{aggregate}},

where $P_{c,\text{screen}} = 10^{-4}$ is the screening threshold and $P_{c,\text{aggregate}}$ is the constellation-level aggregate risk budget (Open Problem OP4).

