8.2 Common-Cause Failure Analysis

NASA guidance states that "the most insidious failure mode is the result of a software common mode failure in which every instance of the same software image executing on multiple devices encounters the same unexpected condition simultaneously." Mitigations:

Mitigation 8.1. Deploy DSK in a dissimilar environment: different OS (e.g., seL4 with formal proof), different compiler, different memory domain, diverse algorithms for critical functions, formal dissimilarity proof.

Mitigation 8.2. Independent hardware monitor: FPGA-based watchdog with formally verified specification that can veto any deployment command.

Mitigation 8.3. N-version voting with known consensus failure modes, using algorithms from independent specification teams.

8.3 Disposal Policy (Corrected)

Obligation 8.1 (FCC 5-Year Rule). The FCC adopted the 5-year rule in September 2022 (FCC 22-74), requiring non-geostationary satellite operators to deorbit satellites within five years after the completion of their missions. The rule officially went into effect on September 29, 2024, and applies to satellites launched on or after that date. Satellites licensed before that date remain grandfathered under the prior 25-year guideline. Graveyard orbit remains a GEO disposal option, not a standard LEO option.

